    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-253511",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Consider the set $A=[0,2].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le 0$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,0]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $0$ is also the minimum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ bound of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(A)=0\\in A.$",
